# Day 7 - Docker Compose (Multi-Container Applications)

## Commands Learned

docker-compose up -d
→ Start all services defined in docker-compose.yml

docker-compose down
→ Stop and remove all containers

docker ps
→ Show running containers

docker logs <container_name>
→ View logs of a container

## Key Concepts

Docker Compose = Tool to run multiple containers using a single configuration file

Service = A container defined inside docker-compose.yml

Multi-container application = Application made of multiple services (e.g., app + database)

## Example docker-compose.yml

version: "3.8"

services:
redis:
image: redis:7-alpine
container_name: redis_server
ports:
- "6379:6379"
volumes:
- redis_data:/data

view_count:
image: thecloudworld/view_count
container_name: view_count_app
depends_on:
- redis
ports:
- "8000:8000"
environment:
- REDIS_HOST=redis
- REDIS_PORT=6379

volumes:
redis_data:

## Explanation

redis service
→ Runs Redis database container

view_count service
→ Runs application container

depends_on
→ Ensures Redis starts before the app

ports
→ Maps host port to container port

environment
→ Passes configuration (Redis host and port to app)

volumes
→ Stores data persistently even if container is removed

## Observations

* Multiple containers run together using one command
* Containers communicate using service names (e.g., redis)
* If one service (like Redis) stops, the app may fail
* Data stored in volumes is not lost after container removal

## Communication Flow

User (Browser → localhost:8000)
↓
Flask App (view_count container)
↓
Redis Container (stores data)
↓
Response (view count displayed)

## Debugging Learned

* Check running containers → docker ps
* View logs → docker logs view_count_app
* Restart services → docker-compose down && docker-compose up -d
* If app not working → check dependent services (like Redis)

## What I Understood

* Docker Compose simplifies multi-container setup
* Services work together to form a complete application
* Containers communicate using internal networking
* Volumes help persist data

## Real Life Use

* Backend + Database applications
* Microservices architecture
* Running complete systems using a single configuration file
* Used in development and deployment environments
